    "summary": "TLDR: Its about how far ai has reached till now as far as doing individual projects is concerned",
    "body": [
      "# Vibe Design #1: In-house Glass-Cleaner Robot",
      "In this post I look at how far AI has come in designing and building complete robotics projects like this one.",
      "## Letting the AI design it",
      "So far, I've had Adam (in Onshape, using Opus 5.5) design the robot from scratch. I didn't give it any drawings or specs, only the task and a few features I wanted. It first produced a good rough design. Then I asked it to refine that design by adding specific components.",
      "## Checking the AI's math",
      "I still haven't checked the math it did for the blade at the given RPM and voltage. It's a simple calculation, and I want to see whether the AI got it right.",
      "The robot has to stay on vertical glass without sliding down. Suction presses it against the glass, and friction from that pressing force has to hold up the robot's weight:",
      "μ · ΔP · A ≥ m · g",
      "so the pressure difference needed is:",
      "**ΔP ≥ m·g / (μ·A)**",
      "where:\n- ΔP is the pressure difference between the outside air and the space under the robot\n- A is the area under suction\n- μ is the friction coefficient between the wet cloth and the glass\n- m is the robot's mass, and g is gravity (9.81 m/s²)",
      "That pressure difference comes from how fast the air moves where the glass meets the wet wiping cloth. To relate it to air velocity, I'll use an energy balance (Bernoulli's equation) between different points in the airflow:",
      "ΔP ≈ ½ · ρ · v²",
      "where ρ is air density (about 1.2 kg/m³) and v is the air velocity. That gives the airflow the blade needs to produce, which I can then check against the AI's blade design at my RPM and voltage.",
      "## Next steps",
      "Once the mechanics check out, I'll start adding the Electronics.",
      "The mechanical design is quiet promising.",
      "## Due to all the pain caused by all the CAD softwares to me till date, I have made my own version of CAD known as Just jelly CAD. It is all-in-one kind of a software which does wiring to urdf, form modelling to simulation all in one with minimal cost.",
      "I wanted to make a software which is web running based, and API calls are not the bottleneck for any design, I have made it so simple that LLMs can work on the design 10x faster than the Adam on onshape. So, yeah its a small fuck you after these big companies extract 1.5K USD per user, I host it on eyecandy GCP server and the whole team can use it, the good thing is anyone can add features and fix bugs and live a happy life. Plus agents help make good cads from drawing withing 2-3 minutes, plus its involved with my knowledge and experience with fusion and onshape, so its pretty decent",
      "Working with PCBs is seemless and user-friendly without breaking the model much"
    ]
